INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "DOJ indictment and Senate Select Committee social-media findings", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-002", "text": "DOJ indictment alleges that the Russian Internet Research Agency (IRA) operated from at least 2014 to interfere with U.S. political processes and the 2016 presidential election; it names Concord Management and Consulting, Concord Catering, and Yevgeniy Prigozhin as funders/controllers. It alleges false U.S. personas, stolen identities, social-media pages/groups aimed at U.S. audiences, U.S.-based infrastructure to conceal Russian origin, rallies, paid ads, and contacts with unwitting Americans. The indictment describes a translator project using YouTube, Facebook, Instagram, and Twitter, a goal of spreading distrust toward candidates and the political system, Project Lakhta, a September 2016 monthly budget exceeding 73 million rubles (over $1.25m), and Prigozhin control of Concord. Senate Volume II reports that IRA operatives masqueraded as Americans and used targeted ads, falsified news, original content, and platform tools to reach tens of millions, polarize users along societal/ideological/racial lines, provoke real-world events, and covertly support Russia's favored candidate. It finds the IRA sought to harm Hillary Clinton and support Donald Trump, at Kremlin direction; race was the dominant target, with over 66% of Facebook ads/content containing a race-related term, Blacktivist generating 11.2m engagements, and 96% of YouTube content aimed at racial issues/police brutality. It reports roughly $100,000 in Facebook/Instagram ads, versus 61,500 Facebook posts, 116,000 Instagram posts, and 10.4m tweets; paid ads were not the main measure of scope. Senate researchers' platform-data abstract reports more than 30m users shared IRA Facebook/Instagram posts in 2015-17, activity across Facebook, Instagram, Twitter and YouTube, and strategies including African-American voter suppression/misinformation, extreme-right confrontation, and sensationalist/conspiratorial content across the political spectrum. NPR summarizes Senate findings that post-election activity increased: Instagram 238%, Facebook 59%, Twitter 52%, YouTube citations 84%. The Senate notes full scope remains unknown because it relied substantially on companies to identify and provide activity."}
